Notes: Medium = photoreal_live_action; classical Bronze Age epic, naturalistic faces, linen and bronze, Mediterranean light

FADE IN:

EST. WINE-DARK SEA - DAY

Sun glints on open water. A lone wake fades toward a forested island. No smoke rises from any Ithaca shore.

(SOUND: wind over waves, distant gulls)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Tell me, O Muse, of that ingenious hero who travelled far and wide after he had sacked the famous town of Troy.

Waves smash a black hull's ghost-image; men cry out among cattle on a bright shore, then fall silent under a burning sun.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Many cities did he visit, and many were the nations with whose manners and customs he was acquainted; moreover he suffered much by sea.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But do what he might he could not save his men, for they perished through their own sheer folly in eating the cattle of the Sun-god Hyperion.

INT. HALL OF OLYMPIAN JOVE - DAY

A vast colonnaded hall above the clouds. JOVE, mature, broad-shouldered, dark-bearded, in white himation with a golden eagle-brooch, sits foremost among the gods. Ambrosial light falls on polished stone.

(SOUND: low thunder under still air, faint feast murmur)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So now all who escaped death in battle or by shipwreck had got safely home except Ulysses, detained by the goddess Calypso.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All the gods had now begun to pity him except Neptune, who still persecuted him without ceasing.

MINERVA stands near the throne—grey-eyed, tall, helmet set back from dark hair, aegis at her breast, bronze-shod spear in hand.

JOVE
See now, how men lay blame upon us gods for what is after all nothing but their own folly.

JOVE (CONT'D)
Look at Aegisthus; he must needs make love to Agamemnon's wife unrighteously and then kill Agamemnon, though he knew it would be the death of him.

He turns his palm outward; the other gods listen.

JOVE (CONT'D)
For I sent Mercury to warn him not to do either of these things, inasmuch as Orestes would be sure to take his revenge.

MINERVA
Father, son of Saturn, King of kings, it served Aegisthus right. But it is for Ulysses that my heart bleeds.

(SOUND: a soft metallic ring as her spear-butt touches stone)

MINERVA (CONT'D)
A goddess lives there, daughter of the magician Atlas. She keeps trying by every kind of blandishment to make him forget his home.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
So that he is tired of life, and thinks of nothing but how he may once more see the smoke of his own chimneys.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
When Ulysses was before Troy did he not propitiate you with many a burnt sacrifice? Why then should you keep on being so angry with him?

JOVE
My child, how can I forget Ulysses than whom there is no more capable man on earth, nor more liberal in his offerings?

JOVE (CONT'D)
Bear in mind, however, that Neptune is still furious with Ulysses for having blinded an eye of Polyphemus king of the Cyclopes.

JOVE (CONT'D)
Still, let us lay our heads together and see how we can help him to return; Neptune will then be pacified.

MINERVA
If then the gods now mean that Ulysses should get home, we should first send Mercury to the Ogygian island.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Tell Calypso that we have made up our minds and that he is to return. In the meantime I will go to Ithaca, to put heart into his son.

She binds on fair sandals that wax not old, takes her bronze spear, and drops from the heights of Olympus.

EXT. SHORE OF ITHACA - DAY

MINERVA, now in the likeness of a Taphian chief—Mentes—stands where the path meets the sand. Spear in hand, she looks toward the house of Ulysses.

(SOUND: surf on shingle, distant feast laughter inland)

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - DAY

SUITORS lounge on hides, dice and feast. Tables groan with meat and wine not theirs. TELEMACHUS, young, lean, dark-haired, in a fresh linen tunic with sword at his shoulder, sits apart, brooding, two hounds at his heels.

(SOUND: lyre under drunken talk, cups knocked on wood)

He is the first to see the stranger at the gate. He crosses, ashamed a guest should wait, takes Minerva's spear, and leads her in.

TELEMACHUS
Welcome to our house, and when you have partaken of food I will ask you who you are.

Servants bring water, bread, and meats. The suitors swagger in from games and fall to. When they have eaten, Telemachus leans close to the guest.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Sir, I hope you will not be offended at what I am going to say. These men think only of music and of song.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
They are easy enough about the matter, for they have got my estate into their hands, and my mother will not say them nay.

MINERVA
I am Mentes, son of Anchialus, and I am on my way to Temesa. Is this your first visit to Ithaca? Tell me, are you the son of Ulysses?

TELEMACHUS
My mother says I am his son; but it is a wise child that knows his own father.

MINERVA
You are your father's son. I never yet saw any one so like another person as you are like Ulysses.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
But tell me, what is the meaning of all this feasting, and who are these people? What is it all about?

TELEMACHUS
As long as my father was here it was well with us. Now the chiefs from the neighbouring islands and from Ithaca itself are wooing my mother.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
She does not refuse to marry, nor yet does she bring matters to an end. They are eating me out of house and home.

MINERVA
How audacious they are. You are no longer a child; you must think how you may rid your house of them.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Call the Achaean heroes in assembly tomorrow. Then fit out a ship, go and inquire about your father.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Go first to Pylos and then to Sparta. If you hear that he is dead, come home, raise a mound, and give your mother to a husband.

She rises as if to go. Telemachus would detain her with gifts; she is already a sea-eagle flying seaward. Awe holds him.

(SOUND: wings beat once hard; feast noise floods back)

EXT. ASSEMBLY PLACE OF ITHACA - DAY

Rosy-fingered dawn light spills over the open stone meeting ground. TELEMACHUS walks in like a god, two hounds at his heels. Oldest councillors make way as he takes his father's seat.

(SOUND: criers' shouts die out; crowd murmur; hounds' nails click on stone)

AEGYPTIUS, bent double with age, white-bearded, rises first. Grief for a lost son still wet on his face.

AEGYPTIUS
Men of Ithaca, hear my words. From the day Ulysses left us there has been no meeting of our councillors until now.

AEGYPTIUS (CONT'D)
Who then can it be, whether old or young, that finds it so necessary to convene us?

TELEMACHUS takes the speaker's staff from the herald.

TELEMACHUS
Sir, it is I who have convened you, for it is I who am the most aggrieved.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
My grievance is purely personal. The first is the loss of my excellent father. The second is much more serious.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
The sons of all the chief men among you are pestering my mother to marry them against her will.

(SOUND: low uneasy voices among the seated men)

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Day by day they keep hanging about my father's house, sacrificing our oxen, sheep, and fat goats for their banquets.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
No estate can stand such recklessness. We have now no Ulysses to ward off harm, and I cannot hold my own against them.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Have respect to your own consciences and to public opinion. Fear, too, the wrath of heaven.

He dashes the staff to the ground and bursts into tears.

(SOUND: staff crack on stone; a held, pitying silence)

ANTINOUS, tall, dark-curled, insolent in a fine cloak, rises among the suitors.

ANTINOUS
Telemachus, it is your own mother who is the most to blame. For three years she has been fooling us.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
She set up a great web in her room and said she would choose a husband when she had woven a shroud for old Laertes.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
By day she wove, and by night she undid the web. When one of her women told us, we caught her in the act.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
Send your mother away, and bid her marry the man of her father Icarius's choice—or whom she will.

TELEMACHUS
I cannot force the mother who bore me from this house. If you choose to take yourselves off, each to his own, do so.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
If not, then devour my substance; but I will cry to the immortal gods, that Jove may grant me my revenge.

Two eagles swoop from a mountain peak, tear at one another over the assembly, then veer toward the houses. Faces blanch.

(SOUND: eagle screams; wings thrash air over the crowd)

An old man reads the omen: Ulysses is not far, and death is weaving for the suitors. The suitors only laugh. Telemachus asks a ship; Mentor alone speaks for him. The assembly breaks.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - NIGHT

Lamp-light. PENELOPE, tall, dark-haired, careworn, fair as Artemis in grief, sits among her maids. She has heard of the voyage. TELEMACHUS stands firm before her.

(SOUND: loom idle; night wind at the shutters)

PENELOPE
Telemachus, I hear you mean to go abroad and leave me. Your father is dead long since. Do not add your own death to mine.

TELEMACHUS
I must go. The gods and my own heart drive me. Nurse, get me provisions ready, and tell no one till twelve days are past.

NURSE EURYCLEA, aged, grey-haired, in plain dark wool, weeps as she fetches wine and barley-meal in jars.

EXT. ITHACA HARBOR - NIGHT

A black ship waits. MINERVA, in Mentor's likeness—older man, grey-bearded, traveler's staff—musts the crew of young Ithacans. TELEMACHUS comes down with the stores. They cast off under a fair west wind she sends.

(SOUND: sail canvas snaps; oars bite black water; ropes creak)

EXT. SHORE OF PYLOS - DAWN

The sun climbs from the fair sea. Nine guilds of Pylians throng the black sand; smoke from black bulls rises toward Neptune. Nine bulls lie ready for each guild.

(SOUND: surf on shingle, low chant of many voices, fat hissing in fire)

A ship furls sail and drops anchor. TELEMACHUS steps ashore. MINERVA as Mentor leads him toward the throng.

MINERVA
Telemachus, you must not be in the least shy or nervous. Go straight up to Nestor that we may see what he has got to tell us.

TELEMACHUS
But how, Mentor, dare I go up to Nestor, and how am I to address him? I am ashamed to begin questioning one so much older than myself.

MINERVA
Some things will be suggested to you by your own instinct, and heaven will prompt you further.

EXT. SHORE OF PYLOS - DAY

NESTOR, aged knight of Gerene, white-bearded, broad-shouldered under a bordered cloak, sits with his sons. Company turns meat on the spits. They crowd round the strangers, take them by the hand, and bid them sit.

(SOUND: meat sizzling, crowd murmur, waves under the talk)

PISISTRATUS, young, open-faced, near Telemachus's age, seats them on soft sheepskins and pours wine into a golden cup, handing it first to Minerva.

PISISTRATUS
Offer a prayer, sir, to King Neptune, for it is his feast that you are joining.

MINERVA
O thou that encirclest the earth, vouchsafe to grant the prayers of thy servants that call upon thee.

(SOUND: wine taps the sand as offering; fire pops)

MINERVA (CONT'D)
More especially we pray thee send down thy grace on Nestor and on his sons. Lastly, grant Telemachus and myself a happy issue.

She passes the cup. TELEMACHUS prays likewise. They eat until they have had enough.

NESTOR
Now that our guests have done their dinner, it will be best to ask them who they are. Who, then, sir strangers, are you?

NESTOR (CONT'D)
Are you traders? or do you sail the seas as rovers with your hand against every man, and every man's hand against you?

TELEMACHUS
Nestor, son of Neleus, honour to the Achaean name. We come from Ithaca under Neritum.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I am come to hear if you can tell me anything about my father. All the others who fought at Troy have got back home.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
But of Ulysses, even those who say they have heard news of him will not say where he is. Therefore I have come to your knees.

NESTOR
My friend, you recall terrible sufferings we endured. There brave Ajax lies, there Achilles, there Patroclus.

NESTOR (CONT'D)
There too my own dear son. And Ulysses—if grey-eyed Minerva loved you as she loved him, some of these suitors would yet taste death.

TELEMACHUS
I can hardly hope for such good fortune. Heaven has made it otherwise for me and for my father.

NESTOR
I will tell you all I know. We sailed from Troy together as far as Tenedos, then foul winds and quarrels parted the fleet.

NESTOR (CONT'D)
I came safe with my ships. Menelaus I know was delayed in Egypt. Go to him; he is but lately come from abroad, and may have news.

At dawn they sacrifice. Minerva reveals herself as a sea-eagle and is gone. Nestor, awed, sends Telemachus overland with Pisistratus in a chariot toward Lacedaemon.

(SOUND: hooves strike the road; axle creaks; morning birds)

EXT. GATE OF MENELAUS'S HOUSE - DAY

Low-lying Lacedaemon opens under hard sunlight. TELEMACHUS and PISISTRATUS rein sweating horses to a halt at the gate.

(SOUND: hooves clatter on packed earth, distant lyre and feast laughter within)

ETEONEUS, lean servant in a plain tunic, steps out, sees the strangers, and bolts back inside.

INT. HALL OF MENELAUS'S HOUSE - DAY

A vast hall gleams like sun and moon—bronze, gold, amber, ivory, silver. MENELAUS, middle-aged king, fair-bearded, broad-shouldered in rich dyed robes, feasts among clansmen. A bard strikes a lyre.

(MUSIC: bright lyre tune under feast chatter)

ETEONEUS
Menelaus, there are some strangers come here, two men, who look like sons of Jove. What are we to do?

MENELAUS
Eteoneus, son of Boethous, you never used to be a fool. Take their horses out, of course, and show the strangers in that they may have supper.

Servants wash and anoint the guests, bring woollen cloaks and shirts. They take seats beside Menelaus. Bread, meats, and cups of gold arrive.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Fall to, and welcome; when you have done supper I shall ask who you are, for the lineage of such men as you cannot have been lost.

TELEMACHUS leans close to Pisistratus.

TELEMACHUS
Look, Pisistratus, see the gleam of bronze and gold—of amber, ivory, and silver. It is like seeing the palace of Olympian Jove.

MENELAUS
No one, my sons, can hold his own with Jove. But among mortal men—well, there may be another who has as much wealth as I have, or there may not.

(SOUND: lyre fades under his voice)

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I have travelled much and undergone much hardship. It was nearly eight years before I could get home with my fleet.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
But while I was getting great riches, my brother was secretly and shockingly murdered through the perfidy of his wicked wife.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
So that I have no pleasure in being lord of all this wealth. I often grieve for those who perished on the plain of Troy.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Yet grieving as I do for all, I grieve for one more than for any other. No one of the Achaeans laboured so hard as Ulysses.

At the name, Telemachus weeps, holding his purple cloak before his eyes. Helen of Argos enters from her high room—fair, stately, attended by maids with a silver work-basket and golden distaff.

HELEN
Menelaus, do we know who these strangers are? Yet I will tell you what I think. Never did I see such a likeness—this boy is the son of Ulysses.

MENELAUS
I too was thinking so. His hands and feet are like, his hair, the cast of his eye. Just now when I was speaking of Ulysses he wept.

PISISTRATUS
You are right, Menelaus. This is the son of that true hero. Nestor sent me with him as guide.

They weep together for the missing and the dead. Helen mixes a drug into the wine that quiets pain and strife. She tells how Ulysses entered Troy in a beggar's disguise. Menelaus tells of the wooden horse, and how only Ulysses held the chiefs silent when Helen walked round calling their wives' names.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
As for your father, I will tell you what the Old Man of the Sea told me on the shore of Egypt, when I held him fast among his seals.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
He said he had seen Ulysses on an island, weeping, in the halls of the nymph Calypso, who keeps him there by force, without ship or crew.

Joy and pain cross Telemachus's face. Menelaus presses gifts upon him—a double cup, a mixing-bowl of silver—and bids him stay. Days pass in feasting.

INT. PENELOPE'S UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

Lamp-light pools on the bed. PENELOPE sleeps at the gates of dreamland, face still wet from earlier tears. The vision of IPHTHIME stands close—same tall sisterly form, bright veil, calm eyes.

(SOUND: soft night wind frets the shutters; a single lamp flame ticks)

PENELOPE
Sister, why have you come here? Am I then to leave off crying? I have lost my brave husband, and now my darling son has gone off on board of a ship.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
I am even more anxious about him than about my husband. Lest something should happen to him, for he has many enemies bent on killing him.

IPHTHIME
Take heart, and be not so much dismayed. There is one gone with him whom many a man would be glad enough to have stand by his side, I mean Minerva.

(MUSIC: thin consoling string note, almost a breath)

IPHTHIME (CONT'D)
It is she who has compassion upon you, and who has sent me to bear you this message.

PENELOPE
Then tell me also about that other unhappy one—is he still alive, or is he already dead and in the house of Hades?

IPHTHIME
I shall not tell you for certain whether he is alive or dead, and there is no use in idle conversation.

(SOUND: a dry whisper as air rushes through the thong-hole of the door)

The vision thins and is dissipated into nothing. Penelope sits up refreshed and comforted.

EXT. OPEN SEA BETWEEN ITHACA AND SAMOS - NIGHT

Black water under a hard sky. A long ship cuts the channel. Armed SUITORS crowd the benches, spears upright—intent on murdering Telemachus.

(SOUND: oar-looms knock thole-pins; hull hisses through chop)

ANTINOUS stands near the stem-post, cloak snapping, eyes fixed ahead on the dark mid-channel.

EXT. ASTERIS ISLET - NIGHT

A rocky islet rises from the strait. Twin harbours cup either side. The suitors' vessel slides into the lee and ships oars. They set a watch among the rocks and settle into ambush.

(SOUND: keel grate on shingle; ropes flung; men splash ashore in low voices)

(MUSIC: low predatory drone under the surf)

INT. OLYMPUS - COUNCIL HALL - DAWN

Marble columns open on cloud light. JOVE sits throned. MINERVA stands before him—grey-eyed, helmet set back, aegis bright, spear grounded.

(SOUND: distant thunder roll; wind through high colonnades)

MINERVA
Father Jove, I hope there may never be such a thing as a kind and well-disposed ruler any more.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
For there is not one of his subjects but has forgotten Ulysses, who ruled them as though he were their father.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
There he is, lying in great pain in an island where dwells the nymph Calypso, who will not let him go.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Wicked people are now trying to murder his only son Telemachus, who is coming home from Pylos and Lacedaemon.

JOVE
Did you not send him there yourself? Besides, you are perfectly able to protect Telemachus, and to see him safely home again.

JOVE turns to MERCURY—youthful, lithe, winged golden sandals, caduceus wand in hand.

JOVE (CONT'D)
Mercury, you are our messenger, go therefore and tell Calypso we have decreed that poor Ulysses is to return home.

(MUSIC: bright heraldic brass under the cloud-wind)

JOVE (CONT'D)
He is to be convoyed neither by gods nor men, but after a perilous voyage of twenty days upon a raft he is to reach fertile Scheria.

JOVE (CONT'D)
The land of the Phaeacians, who are near of kin to the gods, and will honour him as though he were one of ourselves.

EXT. SKY OVER THE OPEN SEA - DAY

Mercury skims the waves like a cormorant, wand held forward, golden sandals flashing spray.

(SOUND: wind scream past wings; sea hiss under skimming flight)

EXT. CALYPSO'S ISLAND - CAVE MOUTH - DAY

Alder, poplar, and sweet cypress stand thick. A vine heavy with grapes frames a cave mouth. Four rills braid through beds of violet and herbage.

(SOUND: birds clamor; water chuckling in stone channels; loom-shuttle click from within)

Smoke of cedar and sandalwood drifts fragrant. Mercury steps inside.

INT. CALYPSO'S CAVE - DAY

A large fire burns on the hearth. CALYPSO works her loom—immortal beauty, tall, dark hair bound, golden shuttle flashing as she sings. She knows Mercury at once and rises.

(SOUND: fire crackle; her song soft against the loom)

CALYPSO
Why have you come to see me, Mercury—honoured, and ever welcome—for you do not visit me often?

She draws a table of ambrosia beside him and mixes red nectar. Mercury eats and drinks his fill.

MERCURY
Jove sent me; it was no doing of mine; who could possibly want to come all this way over the sea?

MERCURY (CONT'D)
Nevertheless I had to come, for none of us other gods can cross Jove, nor transgress his orders.

MERCURY (CONT'D)
He says that you have here the most ill-starred of all those who fought nine years before the city of King Priam.

MERCURY (CONT'D)
Jove says that you are to let this man go at once, for it is decreed that he shall not perish here, far from his own people.

Calypso trembles with rage; the golden shuttle stills in her fist.

CALYPSO
You gods are hard and jealous beyond all measure. You hate it when a goddess takes a man to her bed openly.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
So now you grudge me Ulysses, whom I saved when Jove had struck his ship with lightning and all his men were drowned.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
I loved him and took care of him, and said I would make him immortal. But since no god can cross Jove, let him go—if Jove insists upon it.

MERCURY
Then send him away at once, and beware of Jove's anger.

Mercury departs. Calypso finds ULYSSES on the shore—weathered Greek warrior, dark-bearded, sharp-eyed, sitting where he always sits, weeping, looking over the barren sea.

(SOUND: surf drag on pebbles; his slow ragged breath)

CALYPSO
My poor fellow, do not go on breaking your heart any longer. I am going to send you away of my own free will.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
Cut long beams and make yourself a large raft with a raised deck. I will put bread, water, and wine aboard, and clothe you well.

ULYSSES
Goddess, I am sure you mean mischief in what you are now saying. You cannot really mean to help me get home.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I will not go on board a raft unless you swear a solemn oath that you mean me no mischief.

CALYPSO smiles, strokes his hand, and swears by Earth and Heaven and the water of Styx. She leads him to her cave; he sits where Hermes sat; nymphs set mortal food before him while she takes nectar and ambrosia.

CALYPSO
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, so you would start home to your own land at once? Good luck go with you.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
If you could know the misery still before you, you would stay here, turn man of the world, and let me make you immortal.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
I flatter myself I am no whit less tall or well-looking than your wife can be. Mortals cannot hold their own against goddesses.

ULYSSES
My dear goddess, do not be angry. I know Penelope is nothing like so tall or good-looking as yourself.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
She is only a woman, whereas you are an immortal. Nevertheless, I want to get home, and can think of nothing else.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If some god wrecks me when I am on the sea, I will bear it. I have already suffered much by land and sea.

They sleep in the hollow cave. At dawn Calypso gives him axe and adze. He fells trees, planes beams, builds a broad raft, sets up mast and sail. On the fifth day she bathes him, clothes him, and sends a warm and gentle wind.

EXT. OPEN SEA - DAY

ULYSSES steers by the stars as Calypso taught him—the Pleiads, the Bear that never bathes in Ocean. Seventeen days he runs. On the eighteenth the shadowy mountains of Scheria rise.

(SOUND: steady wind in sailcloth, water hissing along the hull)

NEPTUNE, returning from the Ethiopians, sees him from the mountains of the Solymi. Rage darkens the god's face.

NEPTUNE
Good heavens, so the gods have been changing their minds about Ulysses while I was away among the Ethiopians.

NEPTUNE (CONT'D)
He is close to the land of the Phaeacians, where it is decreed he shall escape. Still I can give him a good voyage yet.

He gathers clouds, troubles the sea with his trident. Winds leap from their quarters. A monstrous wave crashes down. The raft spins; Ulysses is thrown into the water, mast snapped, sail and yard torn away. He surfaces, drags himself aboard the wreckage, clinging as the winds drive him.

(SOUND: gale howl, timber cracking, sea slamming wood)

(MUSIC: brass-torn storm theme)

For two nights and two days he is driven. When dawn of the third day shows land—wooded shore and calm river mouth—a great wave shatters the last of the raft on rocks. He swims, battered, and Minerva puts it in his mind to catch a rock-ledge, then strike out again for the river.

EXT. RIVER MOUTH OF SCHERIA - DUSK

ULYSSES prays to the river god. The current eases. He crawls ashore, salt-crusted, skin swollen, and collapses under two olive trees grown from one stem, heaping leaves over his naked body. Sleep takes him like death.

(SOUND: river soft over stones; night insects; his breath slowing)

INT. NAUSICAA'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Moonlight lies on well-made folding doors. NAUSICAA, a young Phaeacian princess with clear skin, dark hair loosed for sleep, and fine linen, rests as lovely as a goddess. Two pretty maids sleep near the doorway.

(SOUND: soft night wind under the eaves, distant surf)

MINERVA, in the form of a girl Nausicaa's age, hovers like a breath of wind above the bed.

MINERVA
Nausicaa, what can your mother have been about, to have such a lazy daughter? Here are your clothes all lying in disorder.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Yet you are going to be married almost immediately. Suppose then that we make tomorrow a washing day, and start at daybreak.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Ask your father to have a waggon and mules ready at daybreak, to take the rugs, robes, and girdles.

Minerva fades. The room holds only sleeping girls.

INT. ALCINOUS'S PALACE - ROYAL CHAMBER - MORNING

Morning light finds ARETE, mature queen with composed face and purple yarn, spinning by the fireside. KING ALCINOUS, broad-shouldered, kingly beard, council-ready cloak, is halfway to the door. NAUSICAA stops him.

(SOUND: spindle whirr, fire soft crackle)

NAUSICAA
Papa dear, could you manage to let me have a good big waggon? I want to take all our dirty clothes to the river and wash them.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
You are the chief man here, so it is only right that you should have a clean shirt when you attend meetings of the council.

ALCINOUS
You shall have the mules, my love, and whatever else you have a mind for. Be off with you.

EXT. PHAEACIAN WASHING CISTERNS - DAY

Clear water runs always through stone pits by the sea. The party unharnesses the mules. Clothes go into the water; girls tread and vie until linen comes clean, then spread it on high beach shingle.

(SOUND: rushing cistern water, wet cloth slapped on stone, gulls)

They wash themselves, anoint with olive oil, and eat by the stream. They play at ball. Nausicaa sings. The ball flies wide into the river eddy; the girls shriek.

(SOUND: splash; sharp young laughter and cries)

ULYSSES wakes under the olives. He breaks a leafy branch to hide his nakedness and steps from the thicket like a mountain lion. The maids scatter in terror. Only Nausicaa holds her ground—Minerva puts courage in her heart.

ULYSSES
O queen, I throw myself upon your mercy. Are you a goddess or a mortal woman?

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If you are a goddess, you are most like Diana. If you are mortal, thrice happy are your father and mother, and thrice happy your brothers.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Yesterday was the twentieth day I had been floating. Take pity on me. Show me the town, and give me any old wrap to put about me.

NAUSICAA
Stranger, you appear to be a sensible, well-disposed person. There is no accounting for luck; Jove gives good or ill to every man.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
You shall not want for clothes nor for anything else that a hard-pressed suppliant should receive. I will show you the town.

She calls the maids back. They bathe him in the river, give him cloak and shirt from the wash. Minerva makes him taller and stouter, and thickens his hair like hyacinth. Nausicaa stares.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
Listen, my white-armed maids. This man among the gods who hold Olympus is not come without the will of the immortals.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
If only such a one might be called my husband, living here—but that is as the gods please. Give him food and drink.

When he has eaten, she tells him to follow the waggon toward the city, then wait in Athena's poplar grove while she reaches her father's house, lest rude tongues gossip. She describes the palace and bids him go first to her mother Arete.

EXT. GROVE OF MINERVA NEAR SCHERIA - DAY

ULYSSES prays to Minerva. She hears but will not yet show herself openly, for she still fears her brother Neptune's wrath.

(SOUND: leaves stir without wind; distant city murmur)

INT. ALCINOUS'S PALACE - GREAT HALL - DAY

Bronze walls, blue enamel frieze, golden doors, silver posts. Gold and silver hounds guard the entrance, immortal. Within, ARETE spins purple yarn by the hearth. ALCINOUS sits on his throne. ULYSSES, unseen at first, crosses the hall and clasps Arete's knees.

(SOUND: fire soft; distant loom; a held breath in the hall)

ULYSSES
O queen, I am at your knees. May heaven grant you a happy life with your husband and your children. Grant me an escort to my own country, for I have long suffered far from my friends.

He sits in the ashes of the hearth. Silence holds the room. At last an elder speaks; Alcinous raises the stranger, seats him in a chair of honour, and orders food and wine. When Ulysses has eaten, Alcinous promises a ship on the morrow.

ARETE
Stranger, I shall first ask you who you are. Who gave you those clothes? Did you not say you came here from beyond the sea?

ULYSSES
It would be a long story to tell in full. On the way from Ogygia a goddess's island, Calypso kept me seven years. I built a raft; Neptune wrecked it.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Your daughter found me when I was spent. I besought her; she showed no want of judgment, and gave me food and these clothes.

Alcinous wishes such a man might stay as son-in-law; yet he will not keep him against his will. They sleep.

EXT. PHAEACIAN ASSEMBLY - DAY

Rosy morning light hardens on polished stone near the ships. ALCINOUS leads ULYSSES—weathered, newly strengthened about head and shoulders—to sit side by side.

(SOUND: gulls cry over the harbor; crowd murmur rising)

MINERVA, in the form of a herald, moves man by man through the town.

MINERVA
Aldermen and town councillors of the Phaeacians, come to the assembly and listen to the stranger who has just come to the house of King Alcinous.

ALCINOUS
This stranger wants an escort. Let us draw a ship into the sea—one that has never yet made a voyage—and man her with two and fifty of our smartest young sailors.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
When you have made fast your oars, come to my house to prepare a feast. We will have Demodocus to sing; there is no bard like him.

EXT. PHAEACIAN SHORE - DAY

Fifty-two picked oarsmen haul a new ship into the water. Mast and white sails go aboard; oars bind to thole-pins.

(SOUND: hull grinds on shingle; sail canvas snaps open)

INT. ALCINOUS'S PALACE - GREAT HALL - DAY

Sheep, pigs, and oxen are skinned and dressed. A servant leads in DEMODOCUS, blind, beloved of the muse—lyre hung ready. He is set against a bearing-post.

(SOUND: knives on bone; fire roar; feast chatter)

The company eats. Then the muse takes the bard. His fingers find the strings.

(MUSIC: lyre strikes a fierce heroic mode)

DEMODOCUS
(singing)
Of heroes' feats—and the quarrel of Ulysses and Achilles, fierce words heaped at a banquet—beginning of evil on Danaans and Trojans by Jove's will.

Ulysses draws his purple mantle over his head, hiding tears. When the song pauses he wipes his eyes, uncovers, and pours a drink-offering. Only Alcinous hears the heavy sighs and calls for games.

EXT. PHAEACIAN GAMES FIELD - DAY

A wide dusty course. Competitors strip. Foot races, wrestling, jumping, throwing the disc. Crowds ring the marks.

(SOUND: thousands stamping; starter shout; bare feet pounding earth)

LAODAMAS, Alcinous's son, invites the stranger to try his skill. EURYALUS mocks him as no athlete. Ulysses's eyes flash.

ULYSSES
You have stirred me, sir. I am not unskilled in games, though I have suffered much. Stand back, and watch.

He seizes a discus heavier than the rest and hurls it far beyond all marks. Minerva, in a man's form, marks the throw and cries the distance. Ulysses challenges any man in boxing, wrestling, or running—all but Laodamas, his host's son. Alcinous soothes him with praise of Phaeacian ships and dance, and calls Demodocus again.

INT. ALCINOUS'S PALACE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

Torchlight. DEMODOCUS sings of Ares and Aphrodite snared in Hephaestus's net; the hall laughs. Then Ulysses cuts a choice portion of meat for the bard and asks a new song.

(SOUND: wine poured cup to cup, soft guest murmur)

ULYSSES
Demodocus, there is no one in the world whom I admire more than I do you. Sing without fail the story of the wooden horse which Epeus made with Minerva's help.

The bard sings how the Argives fired Troy and Ulysses fought through the streets to Deiphobus's house. Ulysses weeps as a woman weeps over her dying husband. Alcinous stops the song.

ALCINOUS
Let Demodocus cease his song, for this cannot be pleasing to all. Since our guest came he has never ceased weeping.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Tell us your name, your land, your city, that our ships may convey you there. And tell us why you weep when you hear of the fall of Troy.

INT. ALCINOUS'S PALACE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

ULYSSES sits forward on the guest seat, hands open on his knees. The court leans in. ARETE watches. NAUSICAA stills her hands.

(SOUND: low hall murmur, oil lamps soft hiss)

ULYSSES
King Alcinous, it is a good thing to hear a bard with such a divine voice as this man has.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
There is nothing better or more delightful than when a whole people make merry together, guests sitting orderly to listen.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Now, since you ask the story of my sorrows, firstly I will tell you my name, that you too may know it.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I am Ulysses son of Laertes, renowned among mankind for all manner of subtlety, so that my fame ascends to heaven.

EXT. ITHACA - COAST AND MOUNTAIN - DAY

A rugged island rises from the sea. Forested Neritum stands high. Ithaca squats toward the sunset.

(SOUND: surf on rock, wind in pine)

ULYSSES (V.O.)
I live in Ithaca, where there is a high mountain called Neritum, covered with forests. It breeds brave men, and my eyes know none that they better love to look upon.

INT. ALCINOUS'S PALACE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

Ulysses's face hardens with memory.

(SOUND: embers shift in the hearth)

ULYSSES
The goddess Calypso kept me in her cave, and wanted me to marry her, as did also the cunning Aeaean goddess Circe. They could neither persuade me.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
There is nothing dearer to a man than his own country and his parents. Now I will tell you of the many hazardous adventures I met with on my return from Troy.

EXT. ISMARUS - CITY AND SHORE - DAY

Beached black ships. Smoke climbs from a sacked town. ULYSSES and his CREW, bronze-helmed Achaeans, drive captives and divide booty on the sand.

(SOUND: crackling roofs, distant cries, bronze on wood)

ULYSSES (V.O.)
When I had set sail from Troy the wind took me first to Ismarus, city of the Cicons. There I sacked the town and put the people to the sword.

Crewmen drink deep and slaughter sheep. Ulysses points seaward, urgent. They laugh and stay. Inland Cicons pour down. Battle locks near the ships. As the sun goes down the Cicons get the better. Six men from every ship are lost.

(SOUND: spear-shafts clatter, chariot wheels grind sand)

EXT. LAND OF THE LOTUS-EATERS - DAY

A gentle coast. Three scouts taste the lotus flower from mild natives. They forget the way home. Ulysses drags them weeping to the benches and binds them under the rowing thwarts.

(SOUND: soft surf; men sobbing as they are hauled)

ULYSSES (V.O.)
Whoever ate of that honey-sweet fruit had no more wish to bring back news, nor to return at all. I forced them aboard and we sailed on in sorrow.

EXT. CYCLOPES COAST - DAY

A wooded island offshore holds wild goats. Opposite, the mainland caves of the Cyclopes smoke. Ulysses takes his own ship and twelve of his best men, with a skin of dark wine Maron gave him.

(SOUND: oars in calm water; distant sheep bells in cliff caves)

They enter a great cave strewn with cheeses, pens of lambs and kids. The men beg to take cheeses and flee. Ulysses will wait for the master, hoping for gifts.

EXT. CYCLOPES COAST - CAVE MOUTH - DUSK

POLYPHEMUS, a monstrous solitary giant, drives his flocks in, heaves a door-stone no twenty-two wagons could budge, milks ewes, and lights a fire. He sees them.

(SOUND: stone grinds shut; sheep bleat; fire roar)

POLYPHEMUS
Strangers, who are you? Are you traders, or rovers who risk their lives to trouble other people?

ULYSSES
We are Achaeans driven off our course from Troy. We come as suppliants to your knees, hoping you will give us a guest's gift.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Have a fear of the gods. We are under Jove, the avenger of suppliants and strangers.

POLYPHEMUS
The Cyclopes do not care about Jove or any of your blessed gods. We are much stronger than they.

POLYPHEMUS (CONT'D)
I shall not spare either you or your companions out of fear of Jove. Tell me where you left your ship.

ULYSSES
Neptune smashed my ship on the rocks at the far end of your island. I and these men escaped.

The Cyclops snatches two men, dashes their brains out, and devours them raw, bones and all. He sleeps. Ulysses almost stabs him, then remembers the door-stone. Dawn: two more men eaten; the flocks go out; the stone seals them in again.

(SOUND: wet crunch of bone; men screaming; stone thunder)

INT. CYCLOPS CAVE - DAY

Ulysses finds a huge olive club drying. He and his men sharpen one end and harden it in the fire. They cast lots for who shall help drive it home. Evening: two more die. Ulysses offers the giant Maron's wine.

ULYSSES
Cyclops, try this wine, now you have had your meal of man's meat. I brought it as an offering, hoping you would pity me.

Polyphemus drinks deep, thrice, and the wine works on his wits.

POLYPHEMUS
Give me more, and tell me your name at once. I will make you a gift that shall please you.

ULYSSES
My name is Noman; this is what my father and mother and all my friends call me.

POLYPHEMUS
Then I will eat Noman last of all his company. That shall be your gift.

He topples backward in drunken sleep, neck twisted, wine and scraps of flesh spewing. Ulysses heats the stake till it glows. Four men help him drive it into the single eye and twirl it as a shipwright bores a timber.

(SOUND: flesh hiss and pop; giant's roar shaking the cave)

(MUSIC: raw screaming brass under the roar)

Polyphemus wrenches out the stake, bellowing for help. Other Cyclopes gather outside.

CYCLOPS VOICES (O.S.)
What ails you, Polyphemus? Is someone driving off your sheep, or trying to kill you by fraud or force?

POLYPHEMUS
Noman is killing me by fraud! Noman is killing me by force!

CYCLOPS VOICES (O.S.)
If no man is assaulting you, then you must be ill; pray to your father Neptune.

They go. Ulysses laughs in his heart. At dawn he binds the rams in threes, tying a man under each middle beast; himself clings under the prize ram. The blinded giant feels the backs as they pass, but misses the men. Outside, Ulysses frees his crew, drives the sheep to the ship, and they row hard.

EXT. SEA OFF CYCLOPES COAST - DAY

From the deck Ulysses cannot hold his tongue.

ULYSSES
Cyclops, the crew you meant to eat was not helpless. Jove and the other gods have paid you out!

Polyphemus hurls a hill-peak; the wave nearly drives them ashore. They row farther. Ulysses shouts again against all counsel.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Cyclops, if any man asks who put your eye out, say it was Ulysses, sacker of cities, son of Laertes, who lives in Ithaca!

POLYPHEMUS
Alas, the old prophecy is fulfilled. I pray you, father Neptune, if I am indeed your son, grant that Ulysses never reach his home.

POLYPHEMUS (CONT'D)
Or if he must, let him come late, in evil case, with loss of all his crew, upon a foreign ship, and find trouble in his house.

Neptune hears. The giant heaves a larger rock; the spray drives them to the goat island where the other ships wait. They divide the sheep; Ulysses gives the great ram to Jove—who will not yet grant him return.

EXT. AEOLIAN ISLAND - SHORE - DAY

A steep isle rides the bright sea, girded by a dark iron wall. ULYSSES's black ships nose into a sheltered beach.

(SOUND: surf against iron-bound rock, distant feast clatter)

INT. AEOLUS'S HALL - DAY

AEOLUS, mature, broad-shouldered, greying curls, rich dyed mantle and gold arm-rings, entertains Ulysses a full month, hearing of Troy.

(SOUND: spit fat hissing, cups set hard on wood)

At departure Aeolus flays a prime ox-hide and works the roaring winds into it as into a sack, binding the mouth with a bright silver thread. Only the West wind is left free.

AEOLUS
The West wind which is fair for you alone shall blow as it chooses.

EXT. SHIP AT SEA - DAY

Nine days of fair run. On the tenth morning a low native coast lifts; stubble fires wink inland. Ulysses, dead beat, falls into a light sleep at the helm.

(SOUND: steady wind in sailcloth, water hissing along the hull)

Amidships, CREWMAN and other sailors cluster by the bound ox-hide sack.

CREWMAN
Bless my heart, how this man gets honoured wherever he goes. See what fine prizes he is taking home, while we come back empty-handed.

CREWMAN (CONT'D)
Quick—let us see what it all is, and how much gold and silver there is in the sack.

The knot slips. Winds howl out in white violence. The coast of home slides away under black water. Ulysses jerks awake, half rises as if to cast himself overboard—then covers his head while the men wail.

(SOUND: winds shrieking from the sack, sail cracking, men sobbing)

(MUSIC: sudden brass-torn gale theme)

EXT. AEOLIAN ISLAND - SHORE - DAY

The battered fleet beaches again. Ulysses and two companions climb as suppliants to Aeolus's threshold.

INT. AEOLUS'S HALL - DAY

Feast light. Aeolus and his family freeze mid-cup.

(SOUND: feast noise cutting off)

AEOLUS
Ulysses, how is it you are back? Did we not speed you carefully on your way?

ULYSSES
My men have undone me; they and a cruel sleep. Heal the harm, my friends, for you have the power.

AEOLUS
Begone from my island at once. It is not right for me to help a man whom the blessed gods abhor. Go, for you come as one hated of heaven.

They sail on in heavy hearts, the wind gone from their hope.

EXT. LAESTRYGONIAN HARBOR - DAY

A calm fjord between steep cliffs. All ships but Ulysses's enter and moor together. Scouts meet a king's daughter at a spring, then her mother, huge as a mountain, who calls the king. The Laestrygonians swarm, spearing men like fish, smashing the close-packed hulls.

(SOUND: screams; timber splintering; water boiling with blood)

Ulysses cuts his own ship's cable and shouts the crew to the oars. They escape alone while the harbor becomes a slaughter-yard.

EXT. AEAEAN ISLAND - SHORE - DAY

The last black ship beaches in a lonely harbor. For two days they lie broken with fatigue. On the third Ulysses climbs a rock and sees smoke inland. He divides the men; EURYLOCHUS leads twenty-two toward the smoke while Ulysses stays with the ship.

EXT. CIRCE'S HALL - CLEARING - DAY

A fair stone house in a forest glade. Mountain wolves and lions fawn on the men like dogs—Circe's drugged victims. CIRCE, ageless goddess, bright eyes, dark hair bound, rich patterned robes, sings at her loom within.

(SOUND: loom shuttle; her clear song; uneasy animal breath)

She bids them in, mixes cheese, barley, and honeyed wine with a fell drug, then strikes them with her wand. They become pigs in form, yet keep men's minds. She pens them, weeping, and throws them acorns.

Eurylochus alone held back, suspects treachery, and runs to tell Ulysses. Ulysses arms and goes. Hermes meets him in the oak-wood in a youth's form, gives him the herb moly—black root, milk-white flower—and instructs him.

INT. CIRCE'S HALL - DAY

Circe gives Ulysses the cup. He drinks but is not transformed. When she strikes with the wand he draws his sword as if to kill her. She shrieks, clasps his knees.

(SOUND: cup clatters; her sharp cry)

CIRCE
Who are you, and from what city? You must be Ulysses, the man of many wiles, who Hermes said would come. Put up your sword and let us to bed, that we may trust each other.

ULYSSES
Circe, how can you bid me be gentle with you, when you have turned my men into pigs? Swear a great oath you will work me no more mischief.

She swears. The handmaids prepare a bath and a feast. Ulysses will not eat until she frees his men. She salves them; they become taller and younger than before. All weep with joy. She bids him bring the rest from the ship. Eurylochus protests in fear; Ulysses's look silences him.

For a full year they feast in Circe's halls. At last the men urge Ulysses to remember home. In her bed at night he asks her leave to go.

CIRCE
Son of Laertes, you shall not stay unwilling. But first you must go to the house of Hades and dread Proserpine, to seek the spirit of Theban Teiresias.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
He alone of the dead has understanding; the rest flit like shadows. The North wind will blow you to Oceanus. Dig a trench, pour offerings, and sacrifice a ram and a black ewe.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
When Teiresias has drunk the blood he will tell you your way and the measure of your return.

EXT. AEAEAN ISLAND - SHORE - DAY

The black ship is hauled into the surf. Mast and sails go up. Sacrificial sheep are driven aboard. Men weep openly as Circe's wind fills the canvas.

(SOUND: hull scrapes shingle, sheep bleat, oars rattle into tholes)

EXT. SHORE OF OCEANUS - NIGHT

Perpetual mist and melancholy night. No sun-ray pierces. The ship is beached. ULYSSES digs a trench a cubit each way, pours honey and milk, then wine, then water, and sprinkles white barley meal.

(SOUND: blade bites wet earth; distant surf without light)

ULYSSES
I pray to the poor feckless ghosts. When I get back to Ithaca I will sacrifice a barren heifer, the best I had.

He cuts the sheep throats. Dark blood runs into the trench. From Erebus the ghosts troop up—brides, young bachelors, worn old men, maids crossed in love, battle-dead in blood-smirched armour. They flit and scream around the trench.

(SOUND: sheep thrash once; blood patters into the pit; ghost-screams thin as wind through reeds)

The first shade steps clear: ELPENOR, young crewman ghost, barefoot, neck set wrong, eyes wide and unburied.

ULYSSES
Elpenor, how did you come down here into this gloom and darkness? You have got here on foot quicker than I have with my ship.

ELPENOR
Sir, it was all bad luck, and my own unspeakable drunkenness. I fell off Circe's roof and broke my neck.

ELPENOR (CONT'D)
By your wife and by Telemachus, do not leave me unwept and unburied. Burn me, build a barrow on the sea shore, and plant over my grave the oar I used to row with.

ULYSSES
My poor fellow, I will do all that you have asked of me.

Then ANTICLEA, his mother, drifts near. Ulysses weeps but bars her from the blood until TEIRESIAS, aged Theban seer, golden sceptre in hand, comes through the throng.

TEIRESIAS
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, why have you left the light of day and come down to visit the dead in this sad place?

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
Stand back from the trench and withdraw your sword that I may drink of the blood and answer your questions truly.

Ulysses sheathes the sword. Teiresias drinks. Colour touches the dead lips.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
You seek your sweet return. The god who shakes the earth will not make it easy, because he is angry that you blinded his dear son.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
Yet you may still reach home, though in evil plight, if you can restrain your own desire and your men's when you near Thrinacia.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
There you will find the cattle and sheep of the Sun. Leave them unharmed and you may yet reach Ithaca, though late and with loss of all your crew.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
If you harm them, I see destruction for ship and men. You may escape yourself, but you will find trouble in your house—insolent men devouring your substance and wooing your wife.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
You shall take revenge on these suitors. Then go inland with an oar until a man says it is a winnowing fan. Plant it, and sacrifice to Neptune.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
Afterwards offer hecatombs to all the gods. Death shall come to you from the sea, gently, in sleek old age, with your people prosperous about you.

Ulysses lets his mother drink. She tells him Penelope endures, Telemachus holds the land in peace, Laertes grieves in the country, and she herself died of longing for him. Thrice he tries to embrace her; thrice she slips through his arms like a shadow.

ANTICLEA
My son, we no longer have flesh and bones. When life leaves the white bones, the spirit flits away like a dream. Seek now the light of day.

The wives and daughters of heroes gather. Then Agamemnon comes, weeping, telling of Clytemnestra's treachery and warning Ulysses to land in secret. Achilles asks of Neoptolemus and rejoices. Ajax stands apart, still angry over the armour of Achilles, and will not speak. Ulysses sees Minos judging, Tityus, Tantalus, Sisyphus, and the wraith of Heracles. Fear of countless dead throngs him; he and his men return to the ship and the Ocean stream.

EXT. AEAEAN ISLAND - SHORE - DAWN

Rosy light. They fetch Elpenor's body, cut firewood, burn him with his armour, raise a cairn, and fix atop it the oar he once rowed with.

(SOUND: axes biting timber, crackle of rising flame, men weeping)

CIRCE comes along the sand with bread, meat, and wine.

CIRCE
You have done a bold thing in going down alive to the house of Hades. You will have died twice, to other people's once.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Stay here the rest of the day. Feast your fill. At daybreak I will tell Ulysses about your course, and explain everything.

EXT. AEAEAN ISLAND - SHORE - NIGHT

Crewmen sleep by the stern cables. Circe leads Ulysses apart above the tide line.

(SOUND: low surf, night insects, a single torch fluttering)

CIRCE
First you will come to the Sirens who enchant all who come near them. They sit in a green field and warble him to death.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Pass these Sirens by. Stop your men's ears with wax that none of them may hear. If you like you can listen yourself.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Bind you upright on a cross piece half way up the mast. Lash the rope's ends to the mast itself. If you beg them to unloose you, they must bind you faster.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
After that you will come to the Wanderers, or else between Scylla and Charybdis. Scylla has six necks and six terrible heads. She will snatch a man with each.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Charybdis thrice a day sucks down the black water and thrice vomits it up. Hug Scylla's rock and lose six men rather than all.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
You will then reach Thrinacia, where the Sun-god pastures seven herds of cattle and seven flocks of sheep. Leave them unharmed, and you may yet come home.

At dawn they embark. Circe sends a fair wind.

EXT. SEA NEAR THE SIRENS - DAY

Ulysses stops the men's ears with soft wax and has them lash him to the mast. As they draw near, the Sirens' voices pour over the water.

(SOUND: unearthly two-voiced song over calm sea; oars steady)

SIRENS (O.S.)
Come hither, renowned Ulysses, honour to the Achaean name, and listen to our two voices. No one ever sailed past us without staying to hear the enchanting sweetness of our song.

Ulysses nods and frowns for release; the men bind him tighter and row harder. The song fades astern. They free him and take the wax from their ears.

EXT. STRAIT OF SCYLLA AND CHARYBDIS - DAY

On one side Charybdis sucks the sea into her terrible vortex, baring black sand at the bottom, then vomits it skyward. On the other, Scylla's cliff. Ulysses arms himself and stands in the prow, forgetting Circe's counsel not to fight.

(SOUND: water roaring down a throat of rock; wind scream in the strait)

They stare at Charybdis. In that moment Scylla snatches six of the strongest men from the deck. Ulysses sees their hands and feet thrashing as she devours them at the mouth of her cave. They cry to him in their last agony—the most piteous sight, he will say, of all his sufferings.

(SOUND: six screams cut short; oars thrashing in panic)

EXT. THRINACIA - SHORE - DAY

A fair island. Cattle and sheep of the Sun graze. Ulysses hears the lowing and remembers Teiresias and Circe. He would sail past; the men, led by Eurylochus, are exhausted and beg to land for a night. Ulysses makes them swear not to touch the herds.

They beach. Night brings a storm. For a month south and east winds pen them. Stores fail. Men fish and hunt birds. Ulysses goes inland to pray; sleep takes him. Eurylochus sways the crew.

EURYLOCHUS
All deaths are bad enough, but nothing is so bad as death by starvation. Let us drive off the best of the Sun's cattle.

EURYLOCHUS (CONT'D)
If we reach Ithaca we will build a rich temple to the Sun. If he wrecks us in anger, I would rather drown at once than waste to death on a desert island.

They slaughter the cattle, wrap the thigh bones in fat, and roast the meat. Ulysses returns to the smell of roasting and cries aloud to the gods. The hides crawl; the meat lows on the spits. Lampetie tells the Sun; the Sun threatens Jove to shine among the dead if he is not avenged. Jove promises a thunderbolt.

On the seventh day the wind falls. They launch. Once open water is under them, Jove massed a dark cloud and struck the ship with lightning. Men are scattered like sea-crows on the waves and die. Ulysses lashes mast and keel together and rides the wreckage.

(SOUND: thunder crack; hull splitting; men crying once on the wind)

The current drags him back toward Scylla and Charybdis. When Charybdis sucks down his spars he clings to a fig tree on her rock until she vomits them up again. He drops, paddles off, and drifts nine days more until he reaches Ogygia and Calypso—who received him kindly, as he has already told.

INT. ALCINOUS'S PALACE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

Torches gutter. The Phaeacian lords sit frozen in their seats, held by the last of the tale. ALCINOUS leans forward on his carved chair.

(SOUND: torch resin pops; a cup settles on stone)

ALCINOUS
Ulysses, now that you have reached my house I doubt not you will get home without further misadventure.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Our guest has packed the clothes, wrought gold, and valuables you brought. Let each present him further with a large tripod and a cauldron.

The chiefs nod. Benches scrape. They go to prepare the gifts.

EXT. PHAEACIAN SHORE - DUSK

The crew stores the last gifts, meat, and drink. On the stern deck they spread a rug and linen sheet. ULYSSES climbs aboard and lies down. The hawser is loosed.

(SOUND: rope rasps free; oars bite water in one heavy stroke)

As the ship pulls out, Ulysses's eyes close into a deep, sweet, almost deathlike slumber.

EXT. OPEN SEA - NIGHT

The Phaeacian ship bounds forward, prow curvetting like a stallion's neck. Dark-blue water seethes in her wake.

(SOUND: wind in taut canvas; water hissing at speed)

EXT. ITHACA - HARBOR OF PHORCYS - DAWN

A sheltered cove between two headlands, an olive at the head, a cave of the Naiads nearby. The Phaeacians lift the still-sleeping Ulysses with his gifts, lay him on the sand, and depart.

Neptune sees the ship returning and complains to Jove. With Jove's leave he turns her to stone within sight of Scheria and roots her to the sea-floor. Alcinous remembers the old prophecy and bids the people sacrifice, and cease conveying strangers.

EXT. ITHACA - HARBOR OF PHORCYS - DAY

ULYSSES wakes and does not know his own land. Mist from Minerva hides the familiar shores. He thinks the Phaeacians have betrayed him and counts his gifts in anguish.

(SOUND: surf; olive leaves rustling)

MINERVA approaches in the form of a young shepherd, delicate as a king's son.

MINERVA
You are in Ithaca, stranger. It has a name even in Troy, they say, which is said to be far from Achaea.

Joy fills him, but he checks it and answers with a cunning Cretan tale of murder and Phoenician sailors. Minerva smiles and takes the form of a tall, fair woman, skilled in handiwork.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
It would take a cunning knave to better you in trickery, even a god. Can you not leave off your arts even in your own country?

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I am Minerva, daughter of Jove, who stand by you in all your toils. We must hide these goods and plan how you are to destroy the suitors.

She scatters the mist. Ulysses knows at last the harbor, the olive, the cave. They store the gifts in the Naiads' grotto and set a stone at the door. Then they sit beneath the olive to plot death.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
First I will change you into a beggar—withered flesh, dim eyes, ragged cloak—so neither wife nor son nor townsman shall know you.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Go to your swineherd, who is loyal. I will fetch Telemachus from Lacedaemon, for the suitors mean to kill him in the strait.

She strikes him with her wand. Flesh shrinks, hair thins to grey, eyes dull; his clothes become a filthy wrap, a worn deerskin, and a staff with a tattered wallet. She flies toward Sparta.

EXT. WOODED TRACK, ITHACA - DAY

ULYSSES, the bent old beggar in filthy rags, grey stubble on a weathered face, climbs the rough track through wooded country over the mountain crest.

(SOUND: wind in dry leaves, stick tapping rock, distant swine grunting)

EXT. EUMAEUS'S YARD - DAY

Spacious stone pig-yards stand on a height, fenced with thorn and close-set oaken posts. EUMAEUS, a lean weathered swineherd in plain wool, middle-aged, dark cropped hair, strong hands, sits before his hut cutting sandals from stout ox-hide.

(SOUND: knife scraping hide, pigs wallowing, flies buzzing)

Four wolf-fierce hounds fly at the stranger. Ulysses sits down at once and looses his stick. Eumaeus rushes through the gate, shouting, and drives them off with thrown stones.

EUMAEUS
Old man, the dogs were likely to have made short work of you, and then you would have got me into trouble.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
The gods have given me quite enough worries. I have lost the best of masters, and am in continual grief on his account.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
But come inside, and when you have had your fill of bread and wine, tell me where you come from, and all about your misfortunes.

INT. EUMAEUS'S HUT - DAY

Eumaeus strews rushes and a shaggy chamois skin. Ulysses sits, pleased at the welcome.

(SOUND: rushes rustling, fire low in the hearth)

ULYSSES
May Jove, sir, and the rest of the gods grant you your heart's desire in return for the kind way in which you have received me.

EUMAEUS
Stranger, though a still poorer man should come here, it would not be right for me to insult him, for all strangers and beggars are from Jove.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
Heaven has hindered the return of him who would have been always good to me. I wish that Helen's whole race were utterly destroyed.

Eumaeus sacrifices two young pigs, cooks them, and sets the meat before Ulysses with wine in an ivy-wood bowl.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
Fall to, stranger, on a dish of servant's pork. The fat pigs have to go to the suitors, who eat them up without shame or scruple.

Ulysses eats ravenously, silent, brooding revenge behind the beggar's face. He spins a long Cretan tale and swears Ulysses is near and will return with the old moon or the new. Eumaeus will not believe, though he treats the stranger kindly. That night Ulysses tests him with a story of a cloak at Troy; Eumaeus covers him with an extra mantle.

EXT. MENELAUS'S FORECOURT - NIGHT

Torchlight. TELEMACHUS lies awake on a pallet. PISISTRATUS sleeps hard. MINERVA steps close to Telemachus alone.

(SOUND: night insects; distant dogs; a soft footfall on stone)

MINERVA
Telemachus, you should not remain so far away from home any longer. They will eat up everything you have among them.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Ask Menelaus to send you home at once. The chief men among the suitors are lying in wait for you in the Strait between Ithaca and Samos.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Sail night and day, and keep your ship well away from the islands. As soon as you get to Ithaca send your ship and men on to the town.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
But yourself go straight to the swineherd who has charge of your pigs. Stay with him for the night, and then send him to Penelope.

She is gone toward Olympus. Telemachus digs his heel into Pisistratus's side.

TELEMACHUS
Wake up Pisistratus, and yoke the horses to the chariot, for we must set off home.

EXT. MENELAUS'S FORECOURT - DAWN

MENELAUS comes from the house. Telemachus asks leave to go. Menelaus will not detain him, brings gifts—a double cup and a silver mixing-bowl—and Helen adds a robe her own hands wrought for his future bride. An eagle flying right with a goose in its talons gives a fair omen. They drive to Pylos; Telemachus boards his ship, takes on THEOCLYMENUS, a fugitive seer, and sails by night past the ambush, making the swineherd's landing as Minerva bade.

INT. EUMAEUS'S HUT - DAY

Dawn light through the doorway. ULYSSES and EUMAEUS ready cold meat and bread. Outside, pigs grunt.

(SOUND: fire crackle, distant pig bells, bare feet on packed earth)

Footsteps approach. The yard dogs do not bark; they fawn and whine with joy.

ULYSSES
Eumaeus, I hear footsteps; I suppose one of your men is coming, for the dogs are fawning upon him and not barking.

TELEMACHUS stands in the doorway—young, travel-worn, spear in hand, dark hair damp with morning air.

Eumaeus springs up. Mixing-bowls slip from his hands and clatter. He seizes Telemachus, kisses his head and both eyes, weeping, embracing him as a father would an only son returned from the dead.

(SOUND: clay bowls striking stone floor)

EUMAEUS
So you are come, Telemachus, light of my eyes that you are. When I heard you had gone to Pylos I made sure I was never going to see you any more.

TELEMACHUS
So be it, old friend, but I am come now because I want to see you, and to learn whether my mother is still at her old home.

EUMAEUS
She is still at the house, grieving and breaking her heart, and doing nothing but weep, both night and day continually.

Telemachus crosses the threshold. Ulysses rises to yield his seat; Telemachus will not have it. They eat. Telemachus asks about the stranger; Eumaeus tells the Cretan tale and puts him in Telemachus's hands. Telemachus fears to take a guest among the suitors, but promises clothes and passage. Ulysses speaks of the insolence in the house and what he would do if he were young. Eumaeus is sent to tell Penelope her son is safe, secretly, away from the suitors.

When the swineherd is gone, MINERVA comes to the hut door in a woman's form, visible only to Ulysses and the dogs. She signs him out.

EXT. EUMAEUS'S YARD - DAY

Minerva touches Ulysses with her golden wand. Ragged flesh fills; jaw darkens with beard; clothes become clean cloak and tunic. She is gone.

(SOUND: a soft rush of air; dogs whine)

INT. EUMAEUS'S HUT - DAY

Ulysses returns. TELEMACHUS stares, afraid he is a god.

TELEMACHUS
Stranger, you are not the same now as a moment ago. You are one of the gods who hold high heaven. Be gracious, that we may offer you acceptable sacrifice.

ULYSSES
I am no god. I am your father, on whose account you suffer many griefs and bear the violence of men.

He kisses his son and lets fall his tears. Telemachus still cannot believe, until Ulysses tells him this is Minerva's doing. Then Telemachus throws his arms about his father, and both weep aloud, louder than sea-birds robbed of their young.

(SOUND: their broken weeping; fire soft in the hearth)

(MUSIC: low strings swelling then held)

When the wave of grief eases, they plan. Telemachus will go to the town at dawn. Ulysses, again as beggar, will follow with Eumaeus. They will endure insults in the hall. At a sign—when Minerva prompts—they will hide the weapons. If the suitors question, Telemachus will say he has taken them from the smoke. They number the suitors—more than a hundred—and Ulysses trusts the gods and the two loyal herdsmen.

Minerva changes him back to the old beggar before Eumaeus returns with news that the suitors' ship has also come in empty-handed, and Penelope is glad.

INT. EUMAEUS'S HUT - DAWN

Rosy light. TELEMACHUS binds on his sandals and takes a strong spear.

(SOUND: fire pops; sandals slap packed earth)

TELEMACHUS
Old friend, I will now go to the town and show myself to my mother. As for this unfortunate stranger, take him to the town and let him beg there.

ULYSSES
Sir, I do not want to stay here; a beggar can always do better in town than country. Take me as soon as I have had a warm by the fire.

Telemachus strides out through the yards, spear in hand, face set, brooding revenge.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - DAY

TELEMACHUS stands his spear against a bearing-post and crosses inside. NURSE EURYCLEA sees him first and bursts into tears. Maids crowd him with kisses. PENELOPE comes from her room, tall, fair, queenly in fresh linen, weeping as she flings her arms about her son.

(SOUND: spear clacks wood; women's cries and hurried feet)

PENELOPE
Light of my eyes, so you are come home again; I made sure I was never going to see you any more. Come, tell me what you saw.

TELEMACHUS
Do not scold me, mother, nor vex me, seeing what a narrow escape I have had. Wash your face, change your dress, go upstairs with your maids.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Promise full and sufficient hecatombs to all the gods if Jove will only grant us our revenge upon the suitors.

He goes to the assembly place, avoids the suitors' false welcome, sits with old friends, and sees to Theoclymenus. Later, in the hall, he tells Penelope what Menelaus learned of Ulysses on Calypso's island. Theoclymenus reads a bird-omen and swears Ulysses is already on his native soil, sowing death. Penelope doubts, yet hopes.

EXT. PATH TO THE TOWN - DAY

ULYSSES, again the beggar, walks with EUMAEUS toward the city. At a fountain they meet MELANTHIUS the goatherd, who insults them, kicks Ulysses, and threatens him. Ulysses considers killing him, then steadies himself. Eumaeus prays that Apollo may strike the goatherd down.

(SOUND: goats bleat; dust underfoot; a boot striking flesh)

EXT. COURTYARD OF ULYSSES - DAY

An old dog lifts his head from a dung-heap—ARGOS, whom Ulysses bred before Troy. Neglected, flea-ridden, he wags his tail and drops his ears at his master's voice, but has no strength to come near. Ulysses wipes away a tear, asks Eumaeus of the dog, and passes inside. Argos dies as soon as he has seen his master in the twentieth year.

(SOUND: a soft dog's sigh; flies; distant feast noise)

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - DAY

Ulysses enters his own hall as a beggar and sits on the ash-wood threshold within the door. Telemachus sends him bread. He goes among the suitors asking alms. They give, some in pity, some in scorn. Antinous alone refuses and, when rebuked, hurls a footstool that hits Ulysses's right shoulder. Ulysses stands firm as a rock and says nothing, only shakes his head, brooding deep.

(SOUND: stool crack on bone; suitors' mixed laughter and hush)

PENELOPE in her room hears and prays. She sends for the swineherd, wishing to speak with the stranger who may have news. Ulysses says he will come to her after sundown, when the suitors are quieter. Evening falls. The suitors feast again. A second footstool flies from Eurymachus; Telemachus rebukes them and they marvel at his sudden boldness.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - NIGHT

Torchlight washes the smooth pavement. IRUS, a hulking tramp in filthy rags, broad-shouldered and soft-bellied, sways in with a beggar's swagger and plants himself opposite Ulysses.

(SOUND: drunken shuffle on stone, distant suitor laughter)

IRUS
Be off, old man, from the doorway, or you shall be dragged out neck and heels.

ULYSSES
My friend, I do you no manner of harm. There is room enough in this doorway for the pair of us.

IRUS
You filthy glutton, you run on trippingly like an old fish-fag. Get ready, and let these people stand by and look on.

ANTINOUS rises laughing, wine cup in hand. The young men crowd around.

ANTINOUS
This is the finest sport that you ever saw. He who is victorious shall have his pick of the goats' paunches.

ULYSSES
Sirs, an old man worn out with suffering cannot hold his own against a young one. Swear none of you will give me a foul blow.

The suitors swear. TELEMACHUS steps forward.

TELEMACHUS
Stranger, if you have a mind to settle with this fellow, you need not be afraid of any one here. I am host, and Antinous and Eurymachus are of the same mind.

Ulysses girds his rags. Stalwart thighs, broad chest, mighty arms bare themselves. Irus trembles as servants gird him by force.

EXT. OUTER COURT GATE-HOUSE - CONTINUOUS

They haul Irus into the middle of the court. The two men raise their hands.

(SOUND: crowd hush, wind off the stones)

Irus hits Ulysses on the right shoulder. Ulysses drives a fist into the neck under the ear. Bone gives. Blood gushes from Irus's mouth.

(SOUND: wet crack of bone, body slam into dust, suitors' roaring laughter)

Ulysses drags him to the gate-house wall, props him up, and sets the staff in his hands.

ULYSSES
Sit here and keep the dogs and pigs off. If you try to make yourself king of the beggars any more you shall fare still worse.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - CONTINUOUS

The suitors salute the beggar. AMPHINOMUS brings bread and wine and speaks kindly. Ulysses warns him that Ulysses is near and the suitors' doom is coming; let him go home while he can. Amphinomus is troubled, yet he cannot leave; Minerva has bound him to die.

(SOUND: sandals on stone, lingering guffaws)

Penelope, prompted by Minerva, descends to show herself to the suitors and heat their hearts. She rebukes Telemachus for allowing the stranger to be ill-used, then beguiles gifts from the wooers. Ulysses is glad at her cunning. When the suitors go to their homes for the night, father and son take the armour down from the walls into the store-room by the light of Minerva's golden glow.

INT. STORE ROOM PASSAGE - NIGHT

ULYSSES and TELEMACHUS haul helmets, round shields, and long spears into the narrow stone passage. Walls, rafters, and crossbeams flush as with living fire.

(SOUND: bronze clink, spear-butts scraping flagstones)

TELEMACHUS
Father, my eyes behold a great marvel: the walls and the supports on which they rest are all aglow as with a flaming fire. Surely there is some god here.

ULYSSES
Hush. Hold your peace and ask no questions, for this is the manner of the gods. Get you to your bed, and leave me here to talk with your mother and the maids.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - LATER

PENELOPE descends, veiled like a queen, beauty edged with long grief. Maids set her silver-and-ivory seat by the fire. MELANTHO, young, sharp-eyed, insolent, turns on Ulysses.

(SOUND: fire rebuilt, tables dragged)

MELANTHO
Stranger, do you mean to plague us by hanging about the house all night and spying upon the women? Be off, or you shall be driven out with a firebrand.

ULYSSES
I will go tell Telemachus what you are saying, you bold hussy. He will have you cut up on the spot.

Penelope rebukes Melantho and bids the stranger draw near. She asks who he is. He gives another Cretan tale, describing Ulysses's clothes and brooch as he left for Troy. Penelope weeps as the snow melts on mountain peaks. She offers him a bath. He will let only an old woman who has suffered as he has wash his feet.

EURYCLEA brings water. As she washes, she feels the scar above the knee—the wound from the boar on Parnassus in Ulysses's youth. She drops his foot with a clang of the basin, joy and grief flooding her.

(SOUND: bronze basin ringing on stone; water splashing)

EURYCLEA
You are Ulysses! My child—I did not know you until I had handled my master all over!

Her eyes go toward Penelope. Ulysses's hand is at the nurse's throat, pulling her close.

ULYSSES
Nurse, would you ruin me? I am home after twenty years. If you tell anyone, I will not spare you when I destroy the other women in this house.

EURYCLEA
My child, you know how firm I am. If you conquer the suitors, I will name the women who dishonour you.

He sends her for more water. Penelope, to whom Minerva has turned aside the recognition, tells her dream of an eagle killing her geese—then sitting on the roof as her husband. Ulysses says the dream is true doom for the suitors. She answers that dreams pass through two gates, horn and ivory, and she is not sure. Tomorrow she will set the contest of the axes and the bow of Ulysses; the man who strings it and shoots through the twelve axes shall have her.

ULYSSES
Do not put off this contest. Ulysses will be here before any of them can string that bow or shoot through the iron.

She goes up to her room and weeps for Ulysses until Minerva sheds sleep over her eyes.

INT. CLOISTER OF ULYSSES'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Undressed bullock hide and sheepskins on the stone. ULYSSES lies wakeful. Maids who lay with the suitors slip out giggling into the dark. He beats his breast.

(SOUND: distant giggling of women; night insects; a single torch hiss)

ULYSSES
Heart, be still. You had worse than this when the Cyclops ate your brave companions. Yet you bore it in silence till cunning got you safe.

MINERVA, in a mortal woman's likeness, hovers above his head.

MINERVA
My poor unhappy man, why do you lie awake? This is your house. Your wife is safe inside it, and so is your son.

ULYSSES
Goddess, how shall I kill these wicked suitors single handed, seeing what a number of them there always are?

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
And if I succeed, where am I to escape their avengers when it is all over?

MINERVA
For shame. Am I not a goddess, and have I not protected you throughout? Even fifty bands could not stop you. Go to sleep.

She sheds sleep over his eyes and is gone toward Olympus.

INT. PENELOPE'S UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

PENELOPE sits up in bed, dark hair loose, face wet. She weeps, then lifts her hands.

(SOUND: soft weeping; wooden bed creak; wind at the shutters)

PENELOPE
Great Goddess Diana, daughter of Jove, drive an arrow into my heart and slay me. Or let some whirlwind snatch me into Oceanus.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
I would fain go beneath the sad earth still looking towards Ulysses only, without yielding to a worse man than he was.

Day breaks at the window.

EXT. COURTYARD OF ULYSSES - DAWN

Clear sky over the court. Ulysses lifts both hands.

(SOUND: sudden thunder from a cloudless heaven; mill-stones grinding)

ULYSSES
Father Jove, since you brought me home after all afflictions, give me a sign from someone waking within, and another from outside.

Thunder rolls. From the mill room a weary MILLER-WOMAN stops her stone.

MILLER-WOMAN (O.S.)
Father Jove, you have thundered from a clear sky. Let this be the very last day the suitors dine in the house of Ulysses.

Ulysses's face hardens with glad certainty. EUMAEUS arrives with three fine pigs. PHILOETIUS brings a barren heifer and goats. Melanthius gibes; Philoetius salutes Ulysses with his right hand and is promised his master's return. The suitors plot again to kill Telemachus, but Amphinomus will not without the gods' signs. They turn to feast.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - DAY

Telemachus sets Ulysses's chair inside the hall and bids him eat. The suitors abuse him; Ctesippus hurls an ox-foot; Telemachus threatens death to the next man who strikes his guest. Athena keeps the suitors wild with laughter that is not their own; Theoclymenus sees the hall running with blood and ghosts thronging the porch, and leaves while he can. They mock him and fall to their last meal.

(SOUND: forced laughter; knives on trenchers; a lyre out of tune)

PENELOPE goes to the store-room and takes from its peg the great bow of Ulysses, weeping as she holds it. She carries it and a quiver of arrows into the hall, her maids behind with a chest of iron and bronze axes.

PENELOPE
Listen to me, you suitors who plague this house. I set before you the bow of godlike Ulysses. Whoever shall most easily string it and shoot an arrow through all twelve axes, him will I follow.

TELEMACHUS tries first, thrice, and would have succeeded on the fourth but for a glance from his father. He sets the bow aside. The suitors take their turns in order, heating and greasing the bow; none can bend it. Antinous proposes they leave it for another day, after sacrifice to Apollo. Ulysses speaks quietly to Eumaeus and Philoetius outside, tests their loyalty, and reveals himself by the scar. They weep and kiss him; he checks them and gives them their parts—Eumaeus to put the bow in his hands and tell the women to bar their doors; Philoetius to bar the courtyard gate.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - DAY

Ulysses asks, as a curiosity, to try the bow. The suitors flare up. Penelope insists a guest may try; Telemachus sends her to her room—he alone will dispose of the bow. She goes up with her maids, and Minerva sheds sleep upon her.

Eumaeus carries the bow to Ulysses. The suitors shout at the swineherd; Telemachus's hard word steadies him. Euryclea bars the women's door. Philoetius bars the court. Ulysses turns the great bow in his hands, testing it for worms as a bard strings a lyre.

(SOUND: a low taut hum as the string settles home)

(MUSIC: a single held string note sharpening to iron)

In one motion he strings it and plucks the string. Thunder from Jove. He takes an arrow and shoots. The bronze point passes clean through all twelve axe-helve sockets and out the other side.

ULYSSES
Telemachus, the stranger sitting in your halls has not disgraced you. The time has come to get supper ready for the Achaeans while there is light—and afterward such entertainment with song and lyre as are the crown of a feast.

He nods. Telemachus girds on his sword, closes his hand on a spear, and takes his stand beside his father in full armour.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
The trial is over. Now I will see if Apollo will grant my prayer and I hit another mark no man has struck.

He aims at Antinous, who is lifting a two-handled cup of wine, never dreaming of murder in the midst of bread. The arrow passes through his throat. The cup drops; blood spurts from his nostrils; his foot kicks the table away.

(SOUND: bowstring thrum; body and bench crashing; first raw scream)

(MUSIC: percussion like bronze shields struck)

The suitors leap up, searching the walls for shields and spears that are no longer there.

EURYMACHUS
Stranger, you shoot at men to your cost. You have killed the best youth in Ithaca. Vultures shall eat you here.

ULYSSES
You dogs, you thought I should never come back from Troy. You have wasted my substance, forced my maids, and wooed my wife while I was yet alive.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
You feared neither the gods nor the just vengeance of men. Now the cords of destruction are made fast upon you all.

Eurymachus calls them to rush the door and get weapons from the town, or fight. He charges; Ulysses drops him with an arrow through the breast. Amphinomus rushes next; Telemachus spears him from behind and leaves the spear in the body, running to the store-room for arms for himself, Eumaeus, Philoetius, and his father.

Melanthius climbs by the side-vents to the store and brings out twelve shields and spears for the suitors before Eumaeus and Philoetius catch him on a second trip. They bind him fast to a pillar in the store-room, to hang in torment till the fighting ends.

Minerva, in Mentor's form, appears. Ulysses calls to her; the suitors threaten Mentor. She becomes a swallow and sits on a roof-beam, still turning the battle. Six suitors hurl spears together; Minerva makes all six miss. Ulysses and his three strike four men down. Again the suitors throw; Telemachus and Eumaeus are lightly wounded. Ulysses's next volley drops more. The floor runs with blood.

(SOUND: spears clattering, men gasping, bronze on bone, tables overturned)

AJAX is not here—but the slaughter is like a feast of death. Athena lifts her man-destroying aegis from the roof. The suitors flee like cattle stung by a gadfly. Ulysses and his men cut them down as eagles stoop on smaller birds. Leiodes the soothsayer clasps Ulysses's knees and is beheaded for his part in the wooing. Phemius the bard and Medon the herald are spared at Telemachus's word and sent into the court.

The hall is still. Bodies lie in blood and dust thicker than fish drawn onto a beach. Ulysses calls for Euryclea. She would raise a cry of triumph; he checks her—it is an unholy thing to glory over slain men. He asks which of the women have shamed the house. Twelve of fifty, she says. He makes them carry out the dead, sponge the tables and the floor, then leads them into the courtyard where Telemachus hangs them in a single ship's cable. Melanthius is dragged out, mutilated, and left for the dogs.

(SOUND: scrubbing brushes; a rope creak; night air sudden after slaughter)

ULYSSES
Bring fire and sulphur; I will purify the hall. Nurse, go tell Penelope to come, and bring her maids.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - LATER

Sulphur smoke clears. Maids who remained faithful throng Ulysses, kissing his head and shoulders, clasping his hands. A sweet longing takes him, and he breaks into tears.

INT. PENELOPE'S UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

EURYCLEA wakes Penelope with the news that Ulysses is home and has killed the suitors. Penelope will not believe—some god has slain them, and Ulysses is far away. Yet she comes down, still doubting.

INT. GREAT HALL OF ULYSSES - NIGHT

PENELOPE sits opposite ULYSSES in the firelight—he against the tall pillar, eyes on the ground, waiting to see if his wife will speak to him. Long silence. Telemachus rebukes her hard heart. She answers she is stunned; she cannot find words or even look at him. If he is truly Ulysses, they have tokens hidden from all others.

ULYSSES
Telemachus, let your mother test me. She will know me presently. But let us think how to hide this slaughter from the town till we can go to the farm.

He and Telemachus and the herdsmen bathe and put on fresh cloaks. Minerva sheds beauty over him again—height, youth, hyacinthine hair. He sits once more opposite his wife.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Strange woman, the gods have given you a harder heart than any other. No other wife could keep aloof when her husband came home in the twentieth year.

PENELOPE
Strange man, I am not proud or scornful, nor am I confounded. I know what you were when you sailed from Ithaca in the long-oared ships.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Euryclea, make up his great bed outside the chamber he built himself. Put the great bedstead for him there, and lay bedding on it.

Ulysses's anger flares—the test she meant.

ULYSSES
Wife, that is a heart-breaking thing you have said. Who has moved my bed? It would be hard even for a skilled man, unless a god came.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
There was the stump of an olive tree in the court, long-leaved, thick as a pillar. I built my chamber round it, and made the bedpost from that stump, inlaying it with gold, silver, and ivory.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I stretched ox-hide thongs coloured purple. There is our secret. No mortal living could shift that bed easily, for it is rooted still—unless someone has cut under the olive's base.

Her knees and heart loosen as she knows the sure tokens. She bursts into tears and runs straight to him, throwing her arms about his neck and kissing his face.

(SOUND: her sob against his shoulder; the fire's soft collapse)

(MUSIC: warm low strings, long-held resolution)

PENELOPE
Do not be angry, Ulysses. The gods gave us sorrow out of envy, who grudged that we should enjoy our youth together and come gently to old age.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Forgive me, that I did not welcome you at once as I do now. I have always feared some man would come and deceive me with his tale.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
But now, since you have told the clear tokens of our bed, which no other mortal has seen, you convince my heart, for all its stubbornness.

They weep together. It would have been dawn before they had their fill of tears, but Minerva holds the night long in the sky and stays the Dawn at Ocean's edge. Ulysses tells her of the trials still ahead—the journey inland with the oar, as Teiresias foretold—and she answers that they will grow old in peace if the gods allow. They go at last to the rooted bed.

INT. CHAMBER OF ULYSSES - NIGHT

After love and talk, they sleep. At morning Minerva rouses them. Ulysses arms; he wakes Telemachus, Eumaeus, and Philoetius to arm also. They leave the town, and Minerva leads them quickly into the country.

EXT. FARM OF LAERTES - DAY

An old orchard and vineyard. LAERTES, aged, bent, in a stained tunic and gaiters of cowhide sewn, works a plant, grief and years upon him. Ulysses sends the others to the house and tests his father with a stranger's tale, until the old man's sorrow breaks him to dust and ashes in his grief. Then Ulysses flings his arms about him and names himself, showing the scar and naming the trees Laertes gave him as a boy—thirteen pear, ten apple, forty fig, and the rows of vines.

(SOUND: leaves in orchard wind; an old man's broken cry)

LAERTES
Father Jove, there are gods still on high Olympus, if the suitors have truly paid for their violence.

He bathes and is clothed; Minerva makes him taller and stronger to the eye. They sit to a meal in the farmhouse.

EXT. ITHACA TOWN AND COUNTRY - DAY

Rumour of the slaughter runs. The kin of the dead gather, led by Eupeithes, Antinous's father, crying for vengeance. Medon and the bard warn them the gods fought for Ulysses; half turn back. The rest arm and march for the farm.

Minerva asks Jove his will. He bids her do as she pleases, but would have Ulysses sworn in peace as king forever, with forgetfulness of the slaughter. She darts down.

EXT. FARM OF LAERTES - DAY

The enemy approach. Ulysses, Telemachus, the herdsmen, and old Laertes arm. Athena again stands as Mentor. Laertes hurls a spear and kills Eupeithes. Ulysses and Telemachus fall on the front rank.

(SOUND: spear-shouts; bronze clash; running feet in dust)

MINERVA
Hold, men of Ithaca! Back from the bitter strife of blood, that you may part quickly without further slaughter!

Terror pale from the goddess takes them. They flee toward the town. Ulysses starts in pursuit with a war-cry, but Zeus hurls a flaming thunderbolt at Minerva's feet.

(SOUND: thunderbolt crack; earth shudder)

MINERVA
Son of Laertes, hold your hand. End this strife of even-handed war, lest the son of Cronos be angry with you.

Ulysses obeys, glad at heart. Minerva, still in Mentor's form, takes oaths of peace between both sides for the years to come.

EST. WINE-DARK SEA - SUNSET

The sun lowers over Ithaca's rugged shore. Smoke rises at last from the chimneys of the house of Ulysses. A single wake of light lies on the water toward home.

(SOUND: evening surf, distant hearth-voices, a night bird)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So the goddess daughter of Jove made peace between them in the likeness of Mentor, both in form and in voice.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And the man of many turns was home, having seen the cities of many men and learned their minds, and suffered many sorrows on the deep.

> FADE OUT.

THE END
